1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,520
 Hey everyone, this is Ryan here and welcome back to our prosthodontic series.

2
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:11,040
 And now we're going to start to talk about partial dentures.

3
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:15,920
 And the things we talked about with complete dentures like essentialist anatomy, vertical

4
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:22,400
 dimension of occlusion, support, stability, retention, all those things still matter here.

5
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:26,280
 But now we'll talk about things exclusive to partial dentures.

6
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:31,560
 And the big difference with partials is that the patient still has some of their teeth.

7
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:37,600
 And depending on what teeth they have, they're given a specific Kennedy classification.

8
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:41,600
 And that's what we'll focus on in this video.

9
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:47,640
 So again, different people are missing different teeth, and the Kennedy classification system

10
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:54,360
 seeks to group certain patterns of essentialism into these different classes.

11
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:59,280
 So Kennedy Class 1 is a bilateral distal extension.

12
00:00:59,280 --> 00:01:06,720
 And a distal extension means that all the teeth distal to a tooth are missing.

13
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:11,960
 So bilateral, meaning that there's a distal extension on both the patient's right side

14
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:14,320
 and their left side.

15
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:18,800
 So this would be an example of Kennedy Class 1, where the patient is missing all of their

16
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:26,160
 teeth distal to both first premolars in this case.

17
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:30,480
 Kennedy Class 2 is a unilateral distal extension.

18
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:37,000
 So only for this patient, they have a distal extension on their right side.

19
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:44,160
 So how I like to remember this is Class 1 has two distal extensions, whereas Class 2 has

20
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:48,840
 only one distal extension.

21
00:01:48,840 --> 00:01:53,520
 Class 3 is a unilateral bounded-adentialist space.

22
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:59,480
 And a bounded-adentialist space is a space of missing teeth that has teeth on either side

23
00:01:59,480 --> 00:02:00,480
 of it.

24
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:07,360
 In other words, a bounded-adentialist space, an-adentialist space that's bounded on both

25
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:10,720
 sides.

26
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:16,760
 And lastly, we have Class 4, which is a bilateral bounded-adentialist space.

27
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:21,240
 And bilateral, in this case, doesn't mean it happens on both sides of the mouth.

28
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:27,520
 It means that the-adentialist space crosses the midline, like in this picture.

29
00:02:27,520 --> 00:02:33,240
 So those are our four classes, but there are some additional rules we need to keep in mind

30
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:36,640
 to help us with classification.

31
00:02:36,640 --> 00:02:39,920
 And these are called Applegate's Rules.

32
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:44,920
 And the first one kind of goes without saying, and it's that the classification should be

33
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:48,480
 assigned after any extractions.

34
00:02:48,480 --> 00:02:52,920
 So in other words, you wouldn't classify a patient until after they got all the necessary

35
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:56,560
 extractions of teeth that they needed.

36
00:02:56,560 --> 00:03:02,480
 Teeth that have a hopeless periodontal prognosis, or teeth with a non-restorable condition, these

37
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:07,200
 are not teeth you're going to include in their Kennedy classification, because in the treatment

38
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:12,240
 plan, they're going to be removed eventually.

39
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:17,240
 Rule number two is that missing third molars are not considered.

40
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:23,000
 So if the patient is missing their upper third molars on both sides, we wouldn't call this

41
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,920
 Kennedy Class 1, because those would not be considered distal extensions.

42
00:03:30,920 --> 00:03:35,720
 And rule number three, abutment third molars are considered.

43
00:03:35,720 --> 00:03:42,360
 So third molars, like this one, that are present and would need to be used as an abutment tooth

44
00:03:42,360 --> 00:03:48,360
 to help support the partial denture, well this one is considered as a tooth bounding

45
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:50,560
 this essentialist space.

46
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:56,520
 So in other words, let's remember that missing third molars do not count against the patient,

47
00:03:56,520 --> 00:04:05,560
 whereas present third molars do count as an abutment tooth towards the classification.

48
00:04:05,560 --> 00:04:11,840
 Alright and now rule number four is that missing second molars are not considered.

49
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:17,440
 And more accurately, this rule says if a second molar is missing, and is not planned to be

50
00:04:17,440 --> 00:04:23,080
 replaced by the partial denture, in other words that tooth is not going to be included

51
00:04:23,080 --> 00:04:28,360
 as a denture tooth in the partial design, then it's not considered as part of a distal

52
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:32,280
 extension, same as for the third molars.

53
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:37,160
 So say the third molars and the second molars are missing, and the partial will be designed

54
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:40,640
 only as far back as the first molars.

55
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:45,520
 Then we don't care about the second molars, and we're not calling a missing second molar

56
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:48,520
 a distal extension.

57
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:53,160
 So at this point you may be thinking, well Ryan, what if there's a distal extension and

58
00:04:53,160 --> 00:04:55,320
 abounded a denture tooth space?

59
00:04:55,320 --> 00:04:59,480
 Well this next rule is probably the most important one of all of them.

60
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:08,680
 And that's the most posterior edentulous area determines the classification.

61
00:05:08,680 --> 00:05:14,880
 Now the most posterior edentulous area determines the classification, while other edentulous

62
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:19,640
 areas are referred to as modifications.

63
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:27,960
 So in this example, we have two distal extensions and one bounded edentulous space.

64
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:35,960
 Now according to rule number five, the distal extensions are tied for being the most posterior.

65
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:41,320
 And distal extensions by definition are always going to be the most posterior edentulous

66
00:05:41,320 --> 00:05:45,840
 area because they're no teeth distal to them.

67
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:52,360
 Now this is convenient because we have a classification for bilateral distal extension, and that's

68
00:05:52,360 --> 00:05:54,800
 Kennedy class one.

69
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:59,360
 Well, but what do we do with this other edentulous area?

70
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:04,800
 Well it's a modification, and how do we classify modifications?

71
00:06:04,800 --> 00:06:09,600
 Well it's nice and simple, thankfully, it's just by how many there are.

72
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:15,880
 So if this is Kennedy class one, and there's only one modification, we're going to call

73
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:20,680
 it Kennedy class one, modification one.

74
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,280
 And that's it.

75
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:27,760
 And rule number seven is basically what we just said, that the extent of modification

76
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:33,560
 doesn't matter, doesn't matter how big it is, how many teeth are involved, only the amount

77
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:40,400
 of additional spaces in addition to what's needed to define the classification.

78
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:45,200
 And we'll go over a lot more practice later in this video so you can really get to practice

79
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:47,480
 these concepts.

80
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:53,880
 And we have one more rule left, rule number eight, is that class four cannot have any modifications

81
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:56,360
 by definition.

82
00:06:56,360 --> 00:07:02,040
 And this is actually completely logical and reinforces all the previous rules.

83
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:10,520
 A class four bilateral bounded edentulous space will always be by definition more anterior

84
00:07:10,520 --> 00:07:16,360
 than any other possible edentulous space that could determine a classification.

85
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:21,880
 There can't be an edentulous space that's more anterior than something that crosses the

86
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:24,040
 midline.

87
00:07:24,040 --> 00:07:31,120
 So class one, class two and class three will always triumph over a class four, so you can

88
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:36,000
 never have a class four with any modifications.

89
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:43,480
 Alright so we covered all four classes and all eight rules, now it's time for some practice.

90
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:48,940
 So go ahead and pause the video and then we'll go over the Kennedy classification for this

91
00:07:48,940 --> 00:07:53,840
 edentulous arch together.

92
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:59,120
 Alright so this is a picture of a partially edentulous upper arch.

93
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:04,280
 And the first thing we're going to consider is rule number three, which is that the abutment

94
00:08:04,280 --> 00:08:08,840
 third molars are considered as part of the classification.

95
00:08:08,840 --> 00:08:13,400
 And just as a side note, you don't have to remember what number rule corresponds to what

96
00:08:13,400 --> 00:08:19,560
 rule, just so you can get an idea of what they all mean when working together.

97
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:24,560
 So the abutment third molars are going to be considered, which makes these two spaces

98
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:27,960
 bounded edentulous spaces.

99
00:08:27,960 --> 00:08:34,400
 Now they're also both equally posterior, they both involve a missing second molar as

100
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:42,440
 their most posterior spot, and note here that the missing second molars do count because

101
00:08:42,440 --> 00:08:45,440
 we have these third molar abutments.

102
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:53,420
 So by rule number five, this would be considered a Kennedy class three, but according to rules

103
00:08:53,420 --> 00:09:00,160
 number six and seven, if we count one of these as towards the class three, we need to count

104
00:09:00,160 --> 00:09:05,880
 the other one as an additional extra space, which would make it a modification.

105
00:09:05,880 --> 00:09:12,840
 So this one would be Kennedy class three, modification one.

106
00:09:12,840 --> 00:09:14,880
 Alright let's try another one.

107
00:09:14,880 --> 00:09:21,920
 Go ahead and pause the video, and then we'll talk about this together.

108
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:27,640
 Alright so this one is a partially edentulous lower arch, and otherwise it's pretty similar

109
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:29,520
 to the last one.

110
00:09:29,520 --> 00:09:36,160
 You notice there are three bounded edentulous spaces this time, and two of them are tying

111
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:42,240
 for being most posterior, in this case involving a missing first molar as being the most posterior

112
00:09:42,240 --> 00:09:45,000
 teeth.

113
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:51,080
 So since we have three bounded edentulous spaces, two of them tying for most posterior, we can

114
00:09:51,080 --> 00:09:56,560
 count one of them being towards the Kennedy class three, and that leaves two additional

115
00:09:56,560 --> 00:10:06,820
 ones left over, so that would make this a Kennedy class three, modification two.

116
00:10:06,820 --> 00:10:14,080
 Alright so go ahead and pause the video, and then we'll go over this one together.

117
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:21,600
 Alright so this is a partially edentulous upper arch, and this one has a distal extension,

118
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:27,680
 which again will automatically win the battle of being most posterior by definition of distal

119
00:10:27,680 --> 00:10:30,040
 extension.

120
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:35,600
 So this, in addition to this one, this would make it a Kennedy class two, remember class

121
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:41,620
 one has two distal extensions, class two has one, and now we're left with a couple bounded

122
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:43,760
 edentulous spaces.

123
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:49,200
 There's one here, and these might be a little bit hard to see, but there's actually a bounded

124
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:52,800
 edentulous space here, and here as well.

125
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:58,320
 And yes, even just one tooth missing makes it a bounded edentulous space.

126
00:10:58,320 --> 00:11:05,080
 So in this instance, we have the distal extension going towards the classification, and three

127
00:11:05,080 --> 00:11:07,360
 modifications left over.

128
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:14,080
 So this is going to be Kennedy class two, modification three.

129
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:17,920
 And we have one more practice problem, go ahead and pause the video, and then we'll talk

130
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:21,720
 about this.

131
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:28,720
 Alright so this one is again a partially edentulous upper arch, and we have a bilateral distal

132
00:11:28,720 --> 00:11:34,640
 extension, which will automatically classify this as class one.

133
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:40,640
 Now you'll notice we have a bilateral bounded edentulous space, which if it occurred by

134
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:47,040
 itself, without this class one back here, this would be class four, but since we already

135
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:54,200
 have our class one, this whole thing becomes just one modification.

136
00:11:54,200 --> 00:12:00,100
 So this may look like it's very complicated, but it actually turns out to be just a Kennedy

137
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:04,000
 class one, modification one.

138
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:11,840
 Alright, so that's it for this video, I hope it was helpful in learning how to apply the

139
00:12:11,840 --> 00:12:18,140
 Kennedy classification in Applegate's rules to determining different patterns of edentulousism.

140
00:12:18,140 --> 00:12:21,200
 Thanks so much for watching everyone, and we'll see you all in the next video.

